  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Accenture Federal Services obligated $2.4B in FY2026 to date across 355 awards, with an average award size of $6.8M. Obligations fell 25.0% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations were $2.5B in FY21, $2.7B in FY22, $2.9B in FY23, $3.4B in FY24, and $3.2B in FY25 before the current-year total of $2.4B in FY26 to date. The Department of Defense is the top buyer in FY2026 to date, obligating $454M, followed by the Department of Energy with $341M and the Department of Education with $338M. Computer Systems Design Services is the dominant market, accounting for $2.1B of obligations in FY2026 to date. Other notable markets include Custom Computer Programming Services at $92.6M and Other Computer Related Services at $91.8M. The Departments of Veterans Affairs and Health and Human Services and the General Services Administration also feature among the top buyers, obligating $251M, $234M, and $206M respectively. The FY2026 to date profile shows strong concentration in IT and professional services work.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How have Accenture Federal Services obligations changed over recent years?",
        "body": "Accenture Federal Services obligated $2.5B in FY21, $2.7B in FY22, $2.9B in FY23, $3.4B in FY24, $3.2B in FY25, and $2.4B in FY26 to date. Obligations fell 25.0% year over year into FY2026 to date. The largest single-year total in this sequence is $3.4B in FY24. The current-year total is partial and reported as FY2026 to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who and what are the biggest buyers and markets for Accenture Federal Services in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $454M, the Department of Energy obligated $341M, and the Department of Education obligated $338M in FY2026 to date. Computer Systems Design Services is the largest market at $2.1B, with Custom Computer Programming Services at $92.6M and Other Computer Related Services at $91.8M. The Department of Veterans Affairs, the Department of Health and Human Services, and the General Services Administration obligated $251M, $234M, and $206M respectively."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Accenture Federal Services make from federal contracts?",
      "a": "Accenture Federal Services obligated $2.4B in FY2026 to date from federal contracts. The company recorded 355 awards and an average award size of $6.8M, with obligations down 25.0% year over year and historical totals of $2.5B in FY21, $2.7B in FY22, $2.9B in FY23, $3.4B in FY24, and $3.2B in FY25."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies buy from Accenture Federal Services?",
      "a": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $454M in FY2026 to date. Other top buyers include the Department of Energy at $341M, the Department of Education at $338M, the Department of Veterans Affairs at $251M, and the Department of Health and Human Services at $234M; the General Services Administration obligated $206M in FY2026 to date."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Accenture Federal Services sell to the government?",
      "a": "Accenture Federal Services sells primarily IT and professional services to the government. Computer Systems Design Services accounts for $2.1B in FY2026 to date, followed by Custom Computer Programming Services at $92.6M and Other Computer Related Services at $91.8M, with additional work in management consulting, data processing and hosting, software publishing, engineering services, and event services."
    },
